    "engine": "knitr",
    "markdown": "---\ntitle: \"Tasks 1 & 2: Interactive plots\"\ntoc: true\n---\n\n## Task 1: Session check-in\n\nThree interesting or exciting things:\n\n1. Learning how to turn regular ggplots into interactive plots using Plotly.\n2. Seeing how tooltips can display custom information when hovering over points.\n3. Understanding how to combine multiple variables into one label.\n\nThree muddy or unclear things:\n\n1. How to format tooltip labels correctly without getting confused by paste/glue.\n2. When to use aes versus putting arguments outside of it.\n3. How shapes and colors are assigned automatically versus manually.\n\n---\n\n## Task 2: Interactive plot\n\n\n::: {.cell}\n\n```{.r .cell-code}\nlibrary(tidyverse)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-stderr}\n\n```\n── Attaching core tidyverse packages ──────────────────────── tidyverse 2.0.0 ──\n✔ dplyr     1.1.4     ✔ readr     2.1.6\n✔ forcats   1.0.1     ✔ stringr   1.6.0\n✔ ggplot2   4.0.1     ✔ tibble    3.3.0\n✔ lubridate 1.9.4     ✔ tidyr     1.3.2\n✔ purrr     1.2.0     \n── Conflicts ────────────────────────────────────────── tidyverse_conflicts() ──\n✖ dplyr::filter() masks stats::filter()\n✖ dplyr::lag()    masks stats::lag()\nℹ Use the conflicted package (<http://conflicted.r-lib.org/>) to force all conflicts to become errors\n```\n\n\n:::\n\n```{.r .cell-code}\nlibrary(plotly)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-stderr}\n\n```\n\nAttaching package: 'plotly'\n\nThe following object is masked from 'package:ggplot2':\n\n    last_plot\n\nThe following object is masked from 'package:stats':\n\n    filter\n\nThe following object is masked from 'package:graphics':\n\n    layout\n```\n\n\n:::\n\n```{.r .cell-code}\n# Technically this isn't necessary; you can run `penguins` and start using it \n# right away since it's a core dataset. This code just puts the dataset in the \n# environment panel so it's easier to see what's in there.\npenguins <- datasets::penguins\n\n# 11 rows are missing data on penguin sex, so we drop them\npenguins <- penguins |> \n  drop_na(sex)\n```\n:::\n\n\n\n::: {.cell}\n\n```{.r .cell-code}\npenguins <- penguins |> \n  mutate(\n    label = paste0(\n      species, \" (\", sex, \" on \", island, \")\\n\",\n      \"Bill depth: \", round(bill_dep, 1), \" mm\\n\",\n      \"Bill length: \", round(bill_len, 1), \" mm\"\n    )\n  )\n```\n:::\n\n\n\n::: {.cell}\n\n```{.r .cell-code}\nhead(penguins$label)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-stdout}\n\n```\n[1] \"Adelie (male on Torgersen)\\nBill depth: 18.7 mm\\nBill length: 39.1 mm\"  \n[2] \"Adelie (female on Torgersen)\\nBill depth: 17.4 mm\\nBill length: 39.5 mm\"\n[3] \"Adelie (female on Torgersen)\\nBill depth: 18 mm\\nBill length: 40.3 mm\"  \n[4] \"Adelie (female on Torgersen)\\nBill depth: 19.3 mm\\nBill length: 36.7 mm\"\n[5] \"Adelie (male on Torgersen)\\nBill depth: 20.6 mm\\nBill length: 39.3 mm\"  \n[6] \"Adelie (female on Torgersen)\\nBill depth: 17.8 mm\\nBill length: 38.9 mm\"\n```\n\n\n:::\n:::\n\n\n\n::: {.cell}\n\n```{.r .cell-code}\npenguin_plot <- ggplot(\n  penguins,\n  aes(\n    x = bill_len,\n    y = bill_dep,\n    color = species,\n    shape = species,\n    text = label\n  )\n) +\n  geom_point(size = 3) +\n  scale_color_manual(values = c(\"darkorange\", \"purple\", \"cyan4\")) +\n  theme_light() +\n  labs(\n    x = \"Bill length (mm)\",\n    y = \"Bill depth (mm)\",\n    color = \"Species\",\n    shape = \"Species\"\n  )\n\npenguin_plot\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output-display}\n![](interactive-plots_files/figure-html/unnamed-chunk-4-1.png){width=672}\n:::\n:::\n\n\n\n::: {.cell}\n\n```{.r .cell-code}\nggplotly(penguin_plot, tooltip = \"text\")\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output-display}\n\n```{=html}\n<div class=\"plotly html-widget html-fill-item\" id=\"htmlwidget-b74892071682624dfaa0\" style=\"width:100%;height:464px;\"></div>\n<script type=\"application/json\" data-for=\"htmlwidget-b74892071682624dfaa0\">{\"x\":{\"data\":[{\"x\":[39.100000000000001,39.5,40.299999999999997,36.700000